  "notes": [
    "THE AUTO-EXPOSURE HUNT WAS THE TORN FRAME. This file's docstring used to say the gain normalisation was needed because the auto-exposure hunt ran at 43.6 counts/frame. That 43.6 is the stride artefact: the occupied capture's adjacent MAD at the correct stride is 0.615 counts, and the empty room's is 0.006. Gain normalisation is still applied, but it is now correcting a sub-count residual, not a 43-count one.",
    "THE DITHER-GRADIENT SUB-CLAIM IS NO LONGER TESTABLE IN THE EMPTY CAPTURE, and this is a limitation of the control, not a result. At the correct stride the empty room's luma sits at 30.1, far below the braille threshold of 100, so it has almost no pixels within +/-2 of the threshold and the near-threshold bins are empty. The empty room can therefore show that the OCCUPIED delta is not camera noise; it cannot show what the camera does to a signal that sits on the threshold. That question belongs to experiments/ascii_motion_threshold.py, which measures it directly.",
    "The --limit flag was ignored by the previous run (it was declared with a default of 300 and never passed to analyse(), so 400 frames were actually used). It is now honoured, and its default was set to 400 -- the frame count the previous run actually used -- so the comparison is not confounded by the flag itself."
